===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|nautical}} The inward curve of the [[topsides]] of some [[ship]] [[hull]]s; the term was also used for rolling stock on railways in Britain.
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1961|month=August|title=The English Electric Type 5 3,300 h.p. "Deltic" diesels for British Railways|journal=Trains Illustrated|page=468|text=The bodysides have a gentle '''tumblehome''', which gives the machine impressive solidarity of appearance, well set off by the new B.R. standard livery of light-skirted green.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=2024 March 20|author=Greg Morse|title=XP64: the train the {{sic}} [that] launched a new style|journal=RAIL|issue=1005|page=46|text=Like the Pullmans, however, there would be a familiar curving '''tumblehome''' and cant rail, and a similar white window surround {{...}}.}}

